A particularly interesting transition occurs when participants stop merely optimizing their individual moves and start collectively deciding what kind of game they want to play.

Competition can become collaboration. Conflict can become performance. An audience can become creators.

Not every game becomes conscious, but many become reflexive: their participants’ understanding of the system changes the system itself.

And perhaps the greatest freedom isn’t choosing which character to play. It’s realizing you can help write the game without denying everyone else their own characters.